===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er,more}}

# Of a purple hue.
#: {{synonyms|en|purpureal|q1=literary, poetic}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV|40
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought!{{...}}Backed by towering hills, the but faintly discernible '''purple''' line of the French boundary off to the southwest, a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
# {{lb|en|US politics}} Not [[predominant]]ly [[red]] or [[blue]], but having a mixture of [[Democrat]] and [[Republican]] support.
#: {{co|en|[[purple state|'''purple''' state]]}}
#: {{co|en|'''purple''' city}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Hal K. Rothman|title=The Making of Modern Nevada|publisher=University of Nevada Press|isbn=978-0-87417-826-5|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=YzgOWKpnBGcC&pg=PA162&dq=purple|page=162
|passage=In the end, Nevada remained the quintessential '''purple''' state. On the maps that television used to illustrate political trends, Republican states were red and Democratic blue. Nevada blended the colors. It had a bright blue core in the heart of Las Vegas, surrounded by a '''purple''' suburban belt. Most of the rest of the state was bright red, especially in the rural counties.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Margalit Fox|title=Lanny Friedlander, Founder of Reason Magazine, Dies at 63|work=w:The New York Times|date=2011-05-07|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/us/07friedlander.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=As Mr. Friedlander conceived it, Reason was neither strictly right-wing libertarian nor strictly left — in modern parlance, neither red nor blue but a '''purple''' amalgam of the two.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309052001/https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/07/us/07friedlander.html}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-05-04|author=w:Frank Bruni|title=Republicans Are Running Wild in My State|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/04/opinion/north-carolina-republicans.html|passage=Political colorists can be promiscuous in calling states '''purple''', but my state is true to that hue. I speak of North Carolina, and I have receipts: While our junior senator, Ted Budd, is a Republican who won election to a first term in 2022 by about three percentage points, our governor, Roy Cooper, is a Democrat who won election to a second term in 2020 by more than four.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230825134149/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/04/opinion/north-carolina-republicans.html}}
# {{lb|en|[[Belgium]]|[[Netherlands]]}} Mixed between [[social democrat]]s and [[liberal]]s.
# [[imperial|Imperial]]; [[regal]].
#* {{RQ:Shelley Rosalind and Helen|page=82|passage=Grovel on the earth: aye, hide / In the dust thy '''purple''' pride!}}
# [[blood-red|Blood-red]]; [[bloody]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=V|scene=vi|page=171|column=2|passage=O may ſuch '''purple''' teares be alway ſhed}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|The Sixth Book of the [[w:Aeneid|Æneis]]|366|lines=133–134|passage=Wars, horrid Wars I view; a field of Blood; / And ''Tyber'' rolling with a '''Purple''' Flood.}}
# {{lb|en|literature}} Of [[language]], [[extravagantly]] [[ornate]], like [[purple prose]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|flowery|overwrought}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 Aug 79|journal=Gay Community News|page=15|author=Rob Schmieder|title=Anything a Man Does|text=A writer who has made a career of churning out thick novels may be expected to write too quickly to notice that patches of her writing are unbearably '''purple'''.}}
# {{lb|en|motor racing}} Of a [[sector]], [[lap]], etc., [[completed]] in the [[fastest]] time so far in a given [[session]].

