===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|historical}} The [[minutes]], or official record, of a negotiation or transaction; especially a document drawn up officially which forms the legal basis for subsequent agreements based on it. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1842|author=Thomas Campbell|title=Frederick the Great and his Times|volume=II|page=47
|passage=Another account says that, on the morning of the 31st of May, the king delivered to the prince-royal the crown, the sceptre, and the key of his treasure and gave him his blessing. The privy-counsillor Vockerodt drew up at his desire a '''protocol''' of the transaction.}}
# {{lb|en|international law|now|_|rare}} An official record of a [[diplomatic]] meeting or negotiation; later specifically, a draft document setting out [[agreements]] to be signed into force by a subsequent formal [[treaty]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1970|author=Matthew Smith Anderson|title=The Great Powers and the Near East, 1774-1923|page=32
|passage=The terms of this '''protocol''' formed the basis for the Treaty of London signed by the British, French and Russian governments on 6 July 1827.}}
# {{lb|en|international law}} An [[amendment]] to an official [[treaty]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* '''2002''', Philippe Sands, ''Principles of International Environmental Law'', p. 917 n. 253:
#*: The 1992 '''Protocol''' amended the definitions of other terms, including ‘ship’, ‘oil’ and ‘incident’: Art. 2.
# The first [[leaf]] of a roll of [[papyrus]], or the official [[mark]] typically found on such a page. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1991|author=Leila Avrin|title=Scribes, Script, and Books|page=146
|passage=They marked the beginning of each scroll with their '''''protocol''''', a practice that continued in the papyrus trade in the Byzantine Empire [...] into the Islamic period, when there were bilingual '''protocols''' in Greek and Arabic.}}
# The official formulas which appeared at the beginning or end of certain official documents such as [[charter]]s, papal [[bull]]s etc. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1985|journal=Archivum Historiae Pontificiae|volume=23|page=14
|passage=The '''protocol''' of the bull contains elements that appear to be formulaic by the time of John XVIII 's pontificate.}}
# {{lb|en|sciences}} The original [[note]]s of [[observation]]s made during an [[experiment]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1931|author=Gye & Purdy|title=The Cause of Cancer|page=194
|passage=The following is an abstract of the '''protocol''' of the experiment: ''Tumour extract.''—A measured 16 c.c. of minced Rous Sarcoma tissue was ground with sand and extracted with 400 c.c. of 0.8-per-cent. saline.}}
# {{lb|en|sciences}} The precise method for carrying out or reproducing a given experiment. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# The official [[rule]]s and [[guideline]]s for [[head of state|heads of state]] and other dignitaries, governing accepted behaviour in relations with other diplomatic representatives or over affairs of state. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Laura Johnson|title=A mwah too far|journal=The Guardian|date=19 Sep 2009
|passage=Even the Queen (for whom the curtsey is a more standard address) was recently treated to an enthusiastic Obama embrace. Her Majesty, who is not normally known for partaking in such public displays of affection, seemed unperturbed by Michelle Obama's disregard for royal '''protocol'''.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} An accepted [[code of conduct]]; acceptable behaviour in a given situation or group. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Guardian|date=16 Jul 2010
|passage=For those uncertain in the '''protocol''' of handshaking a formula for the perfect handshake has been devised by scientists at the University of Manchester.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 December 2|author=Paul Bigland|title=My weirdest and wackiest Rover yet|journal=Rail|page=66|text=[[TfW]] has staff enforcing mask '''protocols''' at all the busy stations and most travellers oblige, albeit some with ill grace.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A set of formal rules describing how to transmit or exchange data, especially across a network. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2001|title=More Shopping|artist=w:Bran Van 3000|feat={{w|Momus (musician)|Momus}}|album=Discosis|passage=Moloch passed the message to the Behemoth / Whose master passed it on to Zebedee / It was sent by Internet, by obscure '''protocols''' / To its recipient, the delicious Miss Gee}}
#* '''2006''', Zheng & Ni, ''Smart Phone and Next-Generation Mobile Computing'', p. 444:
#*: An exception is Jabber, which is designed based on an open '''protocol''' called the extensible messaging and presence protocol (XMPP).
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Eric Lipton; Ephrat Livni|title=Crypto’s Rapid Move Into Banking Elicits Alarm in Washington|work=The New York Times|date=2021-09-05|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/05/us/politics/cryptocurrency-banking-regulation.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Founders of those kinds of platforms argue that they are just building a “'''protocol'''” ultimately led by a community of users, with the computer code effectively running the show.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251105105648/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/05/us/politics/cryptocurrency-banking-regulation.html|archivedate=5 November 2025}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} The set of instructions allowing a licensed medical professional to start, modify, or stop a medical or patient care order. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# {{lb|en|Roman Catholicism}} The introduction of a liturgical [[preface]], immediately following the ''{{w|Sursum corda}}'' dialogue.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Virgilio T.J. Suerte Felipe|title=The Lord’s Supper, Eucharist, Mass… What’s in a Name?{{nb...}}|isbn=9781449060381|pages=45–46|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=glA-RiQxBsIC&pg=PA45|text=This '''protocol''' of the Preface has been constant, with minor modification, from the ''Apostolic Tradition'', of Hippolytus in 215 A.D., the earliest extant text of the Eucharistic Prayer, to the present 2002 ''Missale Romanum''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Michael S. Driscoll; J. Michael Joncas|title=The Order of Mass: A Roman Missal Study Edition and Workbook|isbn=9781568549897|page=213|text=The proper Preface for Eucharistic Prayer II also appears as Common Preface VI. In it, the '''protocol''' and body of the Preface structure are meshed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=Uwe Michael Lang|title=The Roman Mass: From Early Christian Origins to Tridentine Reform|isbn=9781108832458|page=140|brackets=on|text=The celebrant then takes up this response in the first part of the preface itself (also known by the technical term ‘'''protocol'''’): {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|object-oriented programming}} In some programming languages, a [[data type]] [[declare|declaring]] a set of [[member]]s that must be implemented by a [[class]] or other data type.

