# {{lb|en|transitive}} To press (one's lips) in and together so that they [[protrude]].
#: {{syn|en|round}}
#* '''1901''', {{w|Matilde Serao}}, ''The Land of Cockayne'', translator not credited, London: Heinemann, Chapter IV, p. 72, [https://archive.org/details/landofcockayne00seraiala]
#*: The serving Sister '''pursed''' up her lips to remind him of the cloistral rule, almost as if she wanted to prevent any conversation between him and the nun.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1916|author={{w|Leonid Andreyev}}|chapter=An Original|title=The Little Angel and Other Stories|translator=W. H. Lowe|location=New York|publisher=Alfred Knopf|page=85|url=https://archive.org/details/littleangelother00andriala
|passage=Anton Ivanovich '''pursed''' up his lower lip so that his grey moustache pressed against the tip of his red pitted nose, took in all the officials with his rounded eyes, and after an unavoidable pause emitted a fat unctuous laugh.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:Monty Python|title=w:Always Look on the Bright Side of Life
|passage=When you're feeling in the dumps<br>Don't be silly chumps<br>Just '''purse''' your lips and whistle – that's the thing.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=[[w:Robert M. W. Dixon|R.M.W. Dixon]]|title=Australian Languages: Their Nature and Development|chapter=9|page=403|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year_published=2004
|passage={{...}} Yidinj has just one prefix ''dja:-'' 'in the direction of' {{...}}. There is a noun ''djawa'' 'mouth' in a number of neighbouring languages {{...}} and it is likely that this developed into the prefix ''dja:-''. The semantic motivation would be the fact that Aborigines typically indicate direction by pointing with '''pursed''' lips (in circumstances where Europeans would extend a hand or index finger).}}
# To draw up or contract into folds or wrinkles; to pucker; to knit.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|III|3|line=1756-9|text={{...}} thou {{...}} didst contract and '''purse''' thy brow together, / As if thou then hadst shut up in thy brain / Some horrible conceit: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Billy Budd|13|text=Upon hearing Billy's version, the sage Dansker seemed to divine more than he was told; and after a little meditation during which his wrinkles were '''pursed''' as into a point, quite effacing for the time that quizzing expression his face sometimes wore, "Didn't I say so, Baby Budd?"}}
# To put into a purse.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|I|3|line=502|text=And I will go and '''purse''' the ducats straight,}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete|rare}} To [[steal]] purses; to [[rob]].
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|Scornful Lady|I|1|text=Why I'll '''purse'''; if that raise me not, I'll bet at bowling alleys, or man whores: I would fain live by others.}}

