===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|of animals or plants}} To cause to continue or multiply by generation, or successive production.
#* '''June 1879''', William Keith Brooks, ''Popular Science Monthly'' Volume 15 - The Condition of Women from a Zoological Point of View I
#*: A marked bud-variation is of very rare occurrence, but in many cases the tendency of plants raised from seeds to differ from the parents is so great that choice varieties are '''propagated''' entirely by buds. It is almost hopeless to attempt to '''propagate''' a choice variety of grape or strawberry by seeds, as the individuals raised in this way seldom have the valuable qualities of their parents, and, although they may have new qualities of equal or greater value, the chances are of course greatly against this, since the possibility of undesirable variation is much greater than the chance of a desirable sport.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to spread to extend; to impel or continue forward in space.
#: {{ux|en|to '''propagate''' sound or light}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To spread from person to person; to extend the knowledge of; to originate and spread; to carry from place to place; to [[disseminate]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1938|author=w:Hilaire Belloc|title=The Great Heresies|chapter=4
|passage=There began to appear from the East, cropping up now here, now there, but in general along lines of advance towards the West, individuals or small communities who proposed and '''propagated''' a new and, as they called it, a purified form of religion.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1913|author=w:J. B. Bury|title=A History of Freedom of Thought|chapter=3
|passage=The works of the freethinker Averroes (twelfth century) which were based on Aristotle's philosophy, '''propagated''' a small wave of rationalism in Christian countries.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 19, 2011|author=Kerry Brown| title=Kim Jong-il obituary| work=The Guardian| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/19/kim-jong-il| passage=The DPRK '''propagated''' an extraordinary tale of his birth occurring on Mount Baekdu, one of Korea's most revered sites, being accompanied by shooting stars in the sky.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261011190910/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/dec/19/kim-jong-il|archivedate=11 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To multiply; to increase.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|1|1|text=Griefs of mine own lie heavy in my breast, / Which thou wilt '''propagate'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To generate; to produce.
#* {{RQ:De Quincey Works|volume=XIV|page=157|passage=But to [[w:Edmund Burke|[Edmund] Burke]], {{...}} the mere act of movement became the principle or cause of movement. Motion '''propagated''' motion, and life threw off life.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be propagated; to [[travel]].
# {{lb|en|biology|intransitive}} To produce [[young]]; to be produced or multiplied by generation, or by new shoots or plants.
#* {{RQ:Darwin Variation|chapter=XXVIII|text=As pigeons '''propagate''' so rapidly, I suppose that a thousand or fifteen hundred birds would have to be annually killed by mere chance.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|computing}} To take effect on all relevant devices in a network.
#: {{ux|en|It takes 24 hours for password changes to '''propagate''' throughout the system.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To cause to take effect on all relevant devices in a network.
#: {{ux|en|The server '''propagates''' the password file at midnight each day.}}

